| cells where detector ran     | **38/38** |
| cells where detector tripped | **0/38**  |
| max iter-0 divergence pct    | **0.0551%** |
| median iter-0 divergence pct | **0.0203%** |
| min iter-0 divergence pct    | **0.0161%** |
| gpu_d_bytes vs gpu_bytes diff cells | **0/38** (BYTE-IDENTICAL) |
| cells where gpu_d_vs_cpu_bytes exceeds ±0.5% | **2/38** (the SAME B5 cells) |
| median wall overhead vs gpu  | **+12.1%** |
| min wall overhead vs gpu     | +3.5%     |
| max wall overhead vs gpu     | +27.3%    |
| mean wall overhead vs gpu    | +13.3%    |

## The fatal finding

**Iter-0 score divergence does NOT correlate with end-of-loop bytes
divergence.** The 2 cells that B5 documented as bytes-divergent
(`cid22_3637739_e8_d2_0` -0.55%, `cid22_3637739_e9_d2_0` -0.64%) have
iter-0 score divergence of EXACTLY **0.0210%** — indistinguishable from
the 36 non-divergent cells (whose iter-0 divergence sits in the same
0.016-0.055% band).

The 0.5% threshold catches ZERO cells in either direction:
- The threshold was 25× the W44-RECON-DEEP/A7 measured floor on
  CONVERGED scores, but iter-0 scores are dominated by the constant
  ~0.02% measurement floor that exists on EVERY cell regardless of
  convergence behaviour.
- Lowering the threshold to 0.05% would catch 1 cell (1531677 e8 d=5)
  — a cell that DOESN'T bytes-diverge. Pure false positive.
- Lowering to 0.025% would catch ~5 cells, including the 2 B5
  divergent ones and 3 false positives.
- No threshold cleanly separates the 2 true-positive cells from the
  36 non-divergent ones. The signal is not there at iter 0.

The mechanism W44-RECON-DEEP/A7 documented IS real (GPU reduction-tree
order produces ~1e-7 different scores), but its effect on bytes is
**cumulative across iters**: ULP-scale per-iter score perturbations
nudge the gradient-descent trajectory non-linearly, and on rare images
the cumulative trajectory diverges enough that the final `quant_field`
differs by ~1-block-equivalent, producing 0.5-0.6% bytes drift. None
of that is visible at iter 0 — by design, since the buttloop hasn't
run yet.

