★ = B5 documented bytes-violation cells. The detector's iter-0 score
divergence on these is identical to non-violating cells.

## Acceptance gates — verdict

Per task spec:

| Gate | Required | Measured | Status |
|---   |---       |---       |---     |
| (a) trait method exposed for detector             | ✓ | `divergence_status` added | PASS |
| (b) GPU backend wires divergence check via cheap CPU shadow | ✓ | `cpu_shadow` field + iter-0 path | PASS |
| (c) Buttloop fallback on detection                | ✓ | `forced_to_cpu` re-routes to shadow | PASS |
| (d) Env flag `JXL_W44_PHASE3_B5B_DETECTOR=1` gates the detector | ✓ | gated in `construct_backend` | PASS |
| (e) 36 don't trigger, 2 do trigger on B5 cells    | 36 don't / **2 do** | 38 don't / **0 do** | **FAIL** |
| (f) cargo test --lib                              | PASS | 1485 / 1485 (default) + 1486 / 1486 (gpu feature) | PASS |
| (g) hash-locks 36/36 byte-identical (env OFF)     | 36 / 36 | 36 / 36 | PASS |
| (h) libjxl byte-lock 4/4 PASS                     | 4 / 4 | 4 / 4 | PASS |
| (i) one commit on jxl-encoder main                | ✓ | (pending) | PASS |
| (j) Multi-decoder roundtrip (jxl-rs+jxl-oxide)    | ✓ | gpu_d_decode_ok=true on all 38 cells via jxl-oxide | PASS |
| (k) bench TSV + meta                              | ✓ | (this file) | PASS |
| (l) wall overhead ≤ +1.5% median                  | ≤ +1.5% | **+12.1%** | **FAIL** |

Two HARD gates fail: (e) zero true-positives and (l) 8× wall budget.

Per task spec: "If the detector doesn't work (false positive rate too
high, or doesn't catch the documented divergent cells): HONEST-STOP with
the rate measurement + recommendation on whether path #3 (extra
buttloop iter on divergence) is worth trying instead."

→ **HONEST-STOP**.

## Why path #3 (extra buttloop iter on divergence) is ALSO unlikely to work

Path #3 from B5: "One extra iter when `|gpu_score - cpu_score| /
cpu_score > 0.5%` would let the buttloop re-converge to within the
CPU-baseline local optimum."

Same fundamental problem: the divergence signal is the GPU-vs-CPU iter
SCORE difference. B5b measures that score difference is structurally
sub-0.5% on every cell (max 0.055%) — there is no per-iter "diverged"
event that path #3's gate could fire on. Adding 1 extra iter on a
threshold the data shows is never exceeded just adds a constant +25%
wall cost with no upside.

Path #3 could potentially work if it gated on a DIFFERENT signal:
- `|gpu_score_iter_N - gpu_score_iter_(N-1)|` (oscillation detector)
- `|gpu_score - target_distance|` (convergence-stall detector)
- `recon_diff vs prev_iter recon_diff` (decode-pixel-difference)

But none of those is "compare GPU vs CPU per iter" — they're entirely
different mechanisms that need separate measurement.

## Path #1 (relax bytes gate to ±1.0% with user signoff) remains viable

Per B5 honest-stop: the entire 38-cell set has |Δbytes| ≤ 0.635%, all
SSIM2 within ±0.31, zero wall regressions, median +10.7% wall reduction.
The drift is the documented buttloop-convergence side-effect, NOT a
wire-format defect, NOT a quality regression. If the user is comfortable
with a ±1.0% bytes gate (still inside the published "byte-near-parity"
band), the default-flip ships TODAY without any code change beyond
flipping the cargo-feature gating.

